    <string name="help_title">How PlanetFOSS works</string>
    <string name="help_what_t">What PlanetFOSS is</string>
    <string name="help_what">PlanetFOSS collects what blogs, magazines, projects and video channels publish about Linux and free software – in German and English. Every card shows a short preview and leads straight to the original article. You read the full story where it was written.</string>
    <string name="help_cards_t">How a card comes about</string>
    <string name="help_cards">Every few minutes PlanetFOSS reads the feeds of its sources and uses only what the site itself provides for link previews: title, short description, preview image. A dictionary of more than 500 terms puts every article into sections such as Desktop, Distros or Self-hosting; product names with version numbers become topics.</string>
    <string name="help_bundle_t">Bundles</string>
    <string name="help_bundle">When several sources report the same news, PlanetFOSS shows one card instead of several near-identical ones. You open the other sources inside the card; on the topic page every article gets its own card.</string>
    <string name="help_nova_t">✦ Supernova</string>
    <string name="help_nova">A topic covered by at least four sources within a day, in both languages or across different kinds of sites. Burning supernovas stand on top of Latest; if you like, the app notifies you of new ones.</string>
    <string name="help_comet_t">☄ Comet</string>
    <string name="help_comet">A topic picking up speed: three sources within twelve hours. Comets sit in the “In orbit” row above Latest and between the supernovas – some of them become a supernova.</string>
    <string name="help_glow_t">✧ Afterglow</string>
    <string name="help_glow">When a supernova cools down, the topic stays visible for a while before it moves to the archive.</string>
    <string name="help_stars_t">★ Starred</string>
    <string name="help_stars">Articles readers have marked with a star, collected for seven days. You star with the star on every card – no account: the phone solves a small puzzle so bots do not count, and keeps a random token that lets you take the star back. PlanetFOSS does not know who you are.</string>
    <string name="help_sources_t">The solar system</string>
    <string name="help_sources">The list of all sources with language, kind and how much they published this week. Where a source offers a subscription or donations, PlanetFOSS shows “Support …” – the page opens in your browser.</string>
    <string name="help_data_t">Your data</string>
    <string name="help_data">No account, no tracking, no Google services. The app talks to planetfoss.net and to the sites you open. Anything else that leaves the phone is listed under Privacy in the settings.</string>

